 \section{Conclusion}
 Sequential regional task outputs can reveal location despite local storage of precise coordinates. Probabilistic silence must be included in that analysis. BSP provides a maximal scalar gate for both posterior branches and a sequential cap under explicit model and transition conditions. Exploratory comparisons favor it in some settings, while retained participation concentrates on large-region tasks. Replay accuracy under a uniform-prior inference model is not a practical privacy bound: a development-fitted predictor can perform better without any outputs. A more accurately informed attacker can also exceed the client-side cap. The evidence supports a conditional mechanism and reproducible diagnostics, for applications that expose linkable truthful task outcomes. The comparison with encrypted, padded allocation protocols identifies a different observation boundary, while broader novelty and practical benefit require further evidence.
